t even being in a stock you're  effectively  um in something that's like a  government-linked entity companies woke  companies are sort of quasi-controlled  by the government in a way that Bitcoin  never will be and uh in that sort of a  world  um  I I would submit that perhaps the way we  should think of the Bitcoin to equity  rate you know the Benchmark for Bitcoin  Is Not Gold but equities and the  question is why can't there be parity  between Bitcoin and equities why  shouldn't we be talking about something  more like a hundred to one  which of course won't be won't be as  good as it sounds because um the Fiat  money will be worth a lot less and it'll  be taxed pretty heavily and and whatnot  but I think um but I'm still hopeful  that if uh Bitcoin goes up by a factor  of 100 you will uh you'll make um some  money a modest amount of money in uh in  real terms Peter Thiel doesn't spend his  time just thinking what everyone else is  thinking he fancies himself a contrarian  and as he thinks about how big Bitcoin  could be he's reinforcing the idea that  many of us are likely underestimating  not only how big but how important  Bitcoin could be for the world to see  Peter Thiel somebody who's been thinking  about virtual currencies for decades  come out on stage and explicitly state  that he still thought that Bitcoin had  100 plus X upside was absolutely  breathtaking for a room filled with 30  000 bitcoiners but price targets only  matter so much what was even more  impressive was that Peter Thiel  explicitly stating that he's a Bitcoin  believer that he sees the value of the  this digital currency and he thinks it's  an important tool for Humanity as they  pursue freedom in the coming decades  Peter thiel's a bitcoiner and when Peter  Thiel starts to talk about stuff like  this he's now trending on Twitter and  the mainstream media is paying attention  we're lucky to have Peter on our side  and I think that his point about  bitcoin's market cap reaching parity 